ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186440962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1045 "The Arc Length of a Planar Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f0036e42-d6e1-5a92-99f0-f7897f818532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083772 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083772 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083772 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083772 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bb/bb30925ffaca0ce59de7508e0f3710fbb26f3a80dfbd80b689e9ee5a4cf7d699.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}$ for $x\\in [-3,4],$ as shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083772 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2c7c9a6-db2e-5889-8918-df15ae7908cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083772 #edb/ref 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $a=-3,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2x}{4}=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084092 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084092 #edb/ref 1061 1974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084092 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e30a6e68-0f69-5f3c-9066-8d1faed363f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084092 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084093 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084093 #edb/ref 1061 1975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084093 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "503684ad-7ed7-5631-b233-b8294b8c6b14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084093 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084096 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084096 #edb/ref 1061 1979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084096 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a3b431a-28d4-5ffa-84fe-0570a5b35b3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084096 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084236 #edb/ref 1061 26917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83509829-52cc-54fc-aff4-e35b85b466d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084250 #edb/ref 1061 28187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084250 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b91df088-62b4-5fea-901c-a934dc9c4398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084250 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084388 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we're trying to find the **arc length** of a curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b.$ The arc length of a typical function between two points is shown below:\n\n![](images/e7/e7fb3f6e11e0e2b51fc4ecae9827a4e378a7981450775f2b9330057c319688bf.png)\n\nIn this image, the solid line represents the length that we're trying to find, and the dotted line represents the continuation of the curve $y=f(x)$ beyond the points of interest.\n\nThe arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between the points where $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084388 #edb/ref 1065 1006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084388 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c6cef2d6-0611-5e58-b127-47c3b537067e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084388 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084473 #edb/ref 1064 "We've been using the following formula for the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between the points where $x=a$ and $x=b{:}$\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nFirst, we divide the curve into small subintervals. Consider a part of the blue arc that corresponds to one of those subintervals and let the length of that part be $\\Delta{s}$.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b4d9b2e66ae976ac136170f13525240b02f3f10b3887d6b19738088be528ff.png)\n\nWe can now denote the horizontal displacement as $\\Delta{x}$ and the vertical shift as $\\Delta{y}.$ \n\n![](images/7f/7fe992a709634e9be41f8a2c6d12f7bc72c6f9e8105c19f5f5a47e98c3673b06.png)\n\nTo approximate the length $\\Delta{s}$ we apply the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δs\\approx \\sqrt{{(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{Δy}{Δx})}^{2}}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo approximate the total length $L,$ we need to sum up all of the little $\\Delta s$ terms, so\n\n$$\nL \n\\approx \\sum \\Delta s = \\sum \\sqrt{\\left( \\Delta{x} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\Delta{y} \\right)^2}\n= \\sum \\left( \\sqrt{1 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\Delta{y}}{\\Delta{x}} \\right)^2} \\cdot \\Delta{x} \\right).\n$$\n\nWe then take the limit as $\\Delta x, \\Delta y \\rightarrow 0.$ As we do this, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}s & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{'}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nthe sum becomes a definite integral, and our approximation approaches the exact value of $L{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int \\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{'}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084473 #edb/ref 1065 4576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084473 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8eece214-0f4a-5143-bf64-d939687b9276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084473 #edb/ref 1067 "The Intuition Behind the Main Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320054 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{33}{16}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320054 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320054 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "da1c47f5-5e7e-50ce-b14e-b9f3f3f757ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186320054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320620 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320620 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320620 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186320054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320620 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc4fe866-71a8-5d45-8c02-73304c3706b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321050 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321050 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321050 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321050 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [1,2].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321050 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50520627-69a0-5672-92d6-61584ad60ba6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321050 #edb/ref 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}},$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(2)^{2}}]-[\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{16}-(-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392736 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759